# Keep Small Work Small

Before designing or implementing a small change:

1. Inspect relevant repository and organization precedents first. For infrastructure or CI, find one or two comparable internal deployments before designing from scratch.
2. Confirm the existing environment, authentication model, availability needs, deployment convention, acceptance criteria, and effort budget. Ask only questions that materially change the solution.
3. Propose the fewest new components. State operational cost and meaningful trade-offs before implementation.
4. Agree on a test budget based on user-visible behavior and critical security boundaries. Do not add tests for prose, configuration substrings, hypothetical mutations, or implementation details unless they protect a demonstrated risk.
5. Agree on a review budget. Default to one focused review; fix release blockers and record lower-severity hardening separately.

For a low-volume AWS web app, first consider existing shared networking, a public ALB, one private compute target, ACM, the established runtime convention, artifact-based deployment, and focused smoke tests. Deviate when requirements demand it.

Ask before adding an AWS service, substantially exceeding the agreed test budget, or starting another review cycle.

When architecture changes, stop and revise the plan. Remove superseded code, tests, and documentation instead of accumulating both designs.
